  "preamble": "The shift from 'dh' (ð) to 'z' is the voiced counterpart of sibilantization, turning a voiced dental fricative into a voiced sibilant alveolar fricative. It is the regular reflex of Proto-Semitic *ð in Hebrew and Canaanite (zāqān 'beard'), Akkadian (ziqnum) and Ge'ez (zänäb 'tail'), whereas Aramaic turned *ð into d and Arabic preserved it.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Sibilantization",
